   PPaacckkeett ssoouurrccee iinntteerrffaaccee
     Packet sources, such as network interfaces, may request protocol process‐
     ing using the nneettiissrr__ddiissppaattcchh() and nneettiissrr__qquueeuuee() interfaces.  Both ac‐
     cept a protocol number and _m_b_u_f argument, but while nneettiissrr__qquueeuuee() will
     always execute the protocol handler asynchronously in a deferred context,
     nneettiissrr__ddiissppaattcchh() will optionally direct dispatch if permitted by global
     and per-protocol policy.

     In order to provide additional load balancing and flow information,
     packet sources may also specify an opaque source identifier, which in
     practice might be a network interface number or socket pointer, using the
     nneettiissrr__ddiissppaattcchh__ssrrcc() and nneettiissrr__qquueeuuee__ssrrcc() variants.

   PPrroottooccooll nnuummbbeerr ccoonnssttaannttss
     The follow protocol numbers are currently defined:

     NETISR_IP     IPv4

     NETISR_IGMP   IGMPv3 loopback

     NETISR_ROUTE  Routing socket loopback

     NETISR_ARP    ARP

     NETISR_IPV6   IPv6

